{
  "meta": {
    "title": "mail auth toy: basic and KV login strategies with a limiter and revocation, on the real IMAP session at the edge",
    "status": "done",
    "date": "2026-11-03",
    "author": "platform",
    "track": "mail auth"
  },
  "finding": "Basic and KV strategies, on Redis protocol or Cloudflare KV, work behind the existing login port with one contract suite and no SQL tables, but revocation leaks until sessions register at login, and a strategy needs its own guards for what the store can throw.",
  "premise": "Can prebuilt login strategies run at the edge behind login(user, password, address), with no credential tables, a limiter that holds under a race, and revocation that really ends access?",
  "provenance": {
    "source_workflow": "One general-purpose agent on local workerd, with the real session and objects unedited, vendor identical to mail 00e0585. 8 predictions recorded first (01a1a48e-f944 to 01a1a48e-f9a1), all witnessed, and a further local Cloudflare KV pass.",
    "method": "imaplib over a local TCP-to-WebSocket shim, a contract suite through a worker route in workerd, interleaved timing batches, constructor counters for hibernation, byte scans of state files.",
    "verification": {
      "claims_held": 7,
      "refuted": 2,
      "unverifiable": 2
    },
    "tool_limits": "No redis-server on this machine, so Redis means the toy's own RESP server: not Redis's timing and no TLS. Local KV has no edge caches, so production propagation, and with it the revoked-password lag, is unmeasured. Also unmeasured: deployed hash and wake timing, the deployed iteration cap, secret rotation, plan CPU limits, a login in flight during revoke, submission sessions."
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "imaplib logged in, selected and fetched through the real session, with a list from a binding and no credential in D1 or any of 4,786 state files",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "LOGIN 6.8-10.2 ms over 5x20 runs, locally. 256 ms end to end was measured earlier on deployed Cloudflare, so this does not predict production",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Basic 0.08-1.22 percent, RESP 0.09-1.02, local KV 1.23-2.51, against 516-753 percent with no dummy",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "15 races of 50 passed or hashed exactly 10, refusals hashed 0, in 1.0 ms. With refund on success 11-12 correct passwords passed",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Revoking closes every hibernating session on the mailbox within 1 s.",
      "status": "refuted",
      "is_self_correction": true,
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/",
        "text": "Failed. A logged-in unselected session stayed open and read mail, 5 of 5. Registering at login closed 4 of 4 in 16.6-18.6 ms. Closes reached 2.1 s on empty state and 8.2 s on full state, so 1 s did not hold either"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Works over a new connection each login in 83 file lines, 75 without blanks and comments at first count, against the toy's own RESP server, so neither Redis's timing nor TLS was measured",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "15 of 15 on basic, RESP and local Cloudflare KV, plain and wrapped, in workerd",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "The KV strategy can tell unsafe eviction or persistence from the Redis protocol alone.",
      "status": "unverified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/",
        "text": "Not measured: any answer would have come from the toy's own server. ElastiCache restricts CONFIG, and whether INFO is allowed is unknown"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "The toy's first close range of 15-700 ms described local latency.",
      "status": "refuted",
      "is_self_correction": true,
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/",
        "text": "2 of 10 runs missed 1 s on empty state, and closes reached 8.2 s with 6,505 session files. Restart and empty state changed together, so the cause is not separated."
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "A user of 502 bytes or more makes a KV get throw, so the session closes with EOF instead of NO, 5 of 5, at a length the client chooses before logging in. A 501-byte user gets NO.",
      "status": "verified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Refund on success only ever lets extra correct passwords through, so wrong guesses stay under the limit.",
      "status": "unverified",
      "evidence": {
        "source": "toy-auth",
        "locator": "toy-auth/results/",
        "text": "11-12 correct passwords passed; wrong guesses under refund were not counted."
      }
    }
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "Hashing used 5.8-7.3 ms of CPU locally, against a documented 10 ms on the Free plan.",
    "Ten parallel hashes cost the same as ten serial locally, so hashing serializes, and 50 unlimited logins took 338-349 ms.",
    "Close latency varied up to 8.2 s locally with the cause not separated, so no deployed latency follows from this.",
    "The dummy hash hides hash time and not store time: on local KV an unknown user was about 0.1 ms faster than a wrong password in 5 of 5 batches, and production's gap is unmeasured.",
    "Revocation is per mailbox: another valid password's session also closes and reconnects, since session state stores the account and not the user."
  ],
  "body": {
    "signatures": {
      "Login": "(user: string, password: string, address: string) => Promise<string | null>",
      "basicStrategy": "(list: () => string): Login",
      "kvStrategy": "(store: GetStore): Login",
      "GetStore": "{ get(key: string): Promise<string | null> } // key is binary text, one char per octet",
      "workersKvStore": "(kv: { get(key: string): Promise<string | null> }): GetStore // src/workers-kv.ts, 6 lines",
      "limited": "(limiterFor: (address: string) => LimiterPort, login: Login): Login",
      "LimiterPort": "{ take(): Promise<boolean>; refund(): Promise<void> } // take is one synchronous transaction, which holds the race",
      "ImapMailbox": "revoke(): Promise<{ told; closed; failed }>, register(session: string): Promise<void>",
      "ImapSession": "revoked(): Promise<number> // sends BYE, then calls webSocketClose",
      "entry": "{ user, mailbox, salt, iterations, hash }, 184 bytes in JSON, PBKDF2-SHA256, max 100,000 iterations by schema"
    },
    "must_hold": [
      "Register a session at login, not at SELECT, or a logged-in unselected session survives revocation and reads mail.",
      "Hash the client's octets; generators hash UTF-8. octets() truncates above 0xFF.",
      "A user of 502 bytes or more makes a KV get throw and closes the session without hashing: the port needs a length check or a hashed key, plus a contract clause.",
      "A hung store holds the login without a timeout, and a store that is down gives the client EOF, not NO.",
      "Basic has no revocation event. Writing .dev.vars did nothing, a reload closed sockets without BYE while the old password still worked 5 of 5, and only a restart refused it (n=1). Each reload left one stale sessions name.",
      "One KV entry per user means one password per user.",
      "A plain variable's 5 KB limit fits about 27 basic entries; the docs name no separate secret size.",
      "string | null cannot say refused, so a limiter refusal counts toward the 3-strike BYE. Without refund a correct password is refused after 10 attempts in 5 minutes.",
      "imapObjects gives login no env, so strategies import env from cloudflare:workers. The toy's dummy and refundOnSuccess options are test knobs, not port."
    ],
    "code": "scratchpad/toy-auth (src/, src/redis.ts, src/workers-kv.ts, client/, results/) in session d1068c98-86bb-4fa2-8415-63ac37e80e78"
  }
}
